  "response": "Disputes of this kind are seldom resolved by declaring one explanation right and the other wrong; they are resolved by asking which account better predicts the surviving distribution of evidence. The oldest systematic answer still frames the debate: Edward Gibbon attributed Christianity's success to five secondary causes. Any serious reconstruction must concede at least one of two things, and perhaps both: that the movement's distinctive doctrines contributed to its rapid early growth, or that the social conditions of the surrounding society contributed to that growth. That the surrounding conditions did part of the work is indicated by E. R. Dodds's characterization of the third-century Roman world as an age of anxiety, a diagnosis of collective insecurity for which the religious market supplied remedies.\n\nWhere Gibbon proceeded by enumerating five secondary causes, the sociological turn in the study of Christian expansion has run in the opposite direction, beginning from the mechanisms by which recruits are actually recruited rather than from a catalogue of causes. Because Lofland and Stark's 1965 study of a new religious movement had found that converts joined through pre-existing interpersonal ties, Stark went on to apply sociological models of modern conversion to the early Christian movement. That transfer of models yielded a quantitative result: Stark posited that Christianity grew at about 40 percent per decade. He also estimated growth at roughly 70 percent per decade, and the two figures cannot both be correct, so the arithmetic of the curve is itself part of what scholars must adjudicate before they can weigh doctrine against environment.\n\nDemographic shocks occupy a central place in the social-conditions case, since mortality crises test a community's capacity for care and burial. The Antonine Plague struck the Roman Empire between 165 and 180 CE. The Plague of Cyprian struck the Roman Empire during the 160s CE. The documentary record can be dated on its own terms: Pliny the Younger wrote to the emperor Trajan about Christians in Bithynia around 112 CE, decades before the Antonine Plague, and more than a century before the house church at Dura-Europos, which is dated to the 230s CE.\n\nRoger Bagnall analyzed Christian personal names in Egyptian papyri to estimate rates of Christianization. His onomastic analysis found no Christian names in the Egyptian papyri before the fifth century. Adjudication, in the end, turns on whether an explanation survives contact with material of this kind. Doctrinal accounts earn their keep when the texts that preach exclusivity, bodily resurrection, or charity for strangers can be shown to correlate with observable behaviour; environmental accounts earn theirs when the timing of growth tracks plague, urbanization, or the decay of civic cult. Where names in papyri, letters from provincial governors, and excavated meeting-places converge on a single rate and a single pattern of regional unevenness, the hypothesis that predicts that convergence is preferred, and where they diverge, the dispute remains open rather than decided by rhetorical plausibility.",
